Z||000000||fiero_86_se||04-03-2000||01:40 AM||||does anyone know how to fix the temp guage? It seems to be a common problem in fieros...and I do not know how to fix electrical....||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||04-03-2000||01:43 AM||||Here is a like that shows in detail what to do. <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/tempgauge/tempgauge.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/tempgauge/tempgauge.htm</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ggarsk||04-04-2000||11:30 AM||||Ok,what do you do when your temp gauge doesn't read right. On mine the fan will kick in at about 130 degrees. In other words,it reads a little low. Any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||04-04-2000||12:08 PM||||ggarsk,  mine does exactly the same thing.  I even have a new sensor in the head (V6).  O have to believe that either the connector at the engine or the one to the dash is adding a little resistance causing the low reading.  I have already cleaned them both to no avail.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||04-04-2000||12:09 PM||||Does your temp gage needle move if you make a hard turn? I know that sometimes the needle will come loose from the shaft and you need to pull the gage and re-glue the needle back to the shaft. My car did this when I first bought it. You need to be very careful when you do this. I used a 5-minute epoxy. If you use something like superglue (or something as thin) you risk getting glue between the shaft and the faceplate and the gage won't work at all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-04-2000||12:27 PM||||You can verify the fan switch and gauge pretty easy and cheap without tearing things apart or buying unneeded sensors.<P>get one of the small digital "meat" thermometers at the grocer or ____-mart. they are like 10-15$us. (these come in handy for a few things. Like if you work on the AC.) make sure it's the one with the 300F top limit.<P>then get some dielectric grease and put a small line on the block. put the stem of the thermometer in the grease. (Any grease that won't run when hot will work. we don't need fraction of a degree accuracy here.)<P>the more of the stem in contact with the grease and block the better. At least half the stem against the block if posible. hold the stem in the grease until the numbers are stable on the readout.<P>This lets you do 2 things. 1. is make sure where you want the needle if it's come loose. 2. is make sure wether the gauge or the fan switch is wiggy.<P>when you pick a measuring spot try to get near the sensor that you are testing if posible. temperature varries allot from one part of the block to another.||||reg||1
